      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE DESCRIPTION",
          "content": "We report a series of three femoral stem failures, each occurring at the head-neck junction, with all patients experiencing limited and painful ambulation, leading to subsequent revision arthroplasty. All patients were male with high-offset femoral stems and increased head lengths, and each had undergone primary THA at a minimum of 7 years before presentation (average, 94 months). There were no associated deep infections or cases of aseptic loosening in the cohort."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "LITERATURE REVIEW",
          "content": "There is a paucity of similar reports in the literature regarding femoral stem failure at the head-neck junction. When failures of titanium stems have been reported, failure has been attributed to material design and geometry, laser etching, overload, implant alignment, and patient characteristics."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "PURPOSE AND CLINICAL RELEVANCE",
          "content": "Catastrophic failures of femoral stems at the head-neck junction are a rare cause for revision after THA. Component material and design, surgical technique, and patient factors may contribute."
        }
      ]},
